speechtext:
I referred to the auditor general's report as of December 31, 1936.
I do not know what reports have been made since that time. I have not that information, but that is not material. I am not concerned with what happened after that date ;
I am talking about the report as it appears in the auditor general's report.

Surely it is not wrong
to use the word "chosen." For instance, he might have been chosen by Providence;
divine inspiration might have descended upon the hon. member. In view of the hon. member's speech I am surprised he should be so very thin-skinned, because if there is any merit in the hon. member's speech then the Minister of Transport, as he has said himself, ought not to remain in public life. If a man takes the responsibility of making charges of that sort with respect to a minister and a department surely he cannot take exception to the statement that he was chosen to do the work.

I hope my hon. friend is
not accusing any particular branch of the 51952-181}
government, because there are three parties involved, any one of whom might be responsible. The board of railway commissioners has an efficient engineering staff that looks after work of this sort, specializing in grade crossings. The work is carefully planned and supervised, and every precaution is taken against the sort of engineering mistake the hon. gentleman refers to.
